WEBVTT

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:05.398
This is the SlimFaas Live Stream
dashboard. Fibonacci has a route, a queue,

00:00:05.398 --> 00:00:07.682
and right now, zero running replicas.

00:00:09.500 --> 00:00:14.915
One synchronous call asks for Fibonacci of
ten. SlimFaas holds the request while

00:00:14.915 --> 00:00:18.247
Kubernetes starts a pod. The caller gets
fifty-five.

00:00:19.900 --> 00:00:24.568
Watch the replica count. When the idle
timeout expires, Kubernetes scales the

00:00:24.568 --> 00:00:26.513
deployment back down to zero.

00:00:28.200 --> 00:00:32.832
Now thirty clients ask for Fibonacci of
forty-eight. Each gets an accepted

00:00:32.832 --> 00:00:36.307
response while the actual computation
waits in the queue.

00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:42.685
As the backlog grows, the scaling rule
requests more pods. Each ready worker

00:00:42.685 --> 00:00:44.848
takes one computation at a time.

00:00:46.500 --> 00:00:51.631
The queue absorbs the spike so all thirty
computations do not compete for CPU at

00:00:51.631 --> 00:00:56.420
once. When the work drains and traffic
stops, the workers go back to zero.

00:00:58.100 --> 00:01:03.636
Consider an agent system. Two evaluator
replicas and two audit replicas each cache

00:01:03.636 --> 00:01:08.745
a tool catalog. When a tool changes, every
local copy needs invalidating.

00:01:10.400 --> 00:01:15.023
One publish event fans out to both
deployments. All four running replicas

00:01:15.023 --> 00:01:16.949
discard their own cached copy.

00:01:19.200 --> 00:01:23.826
A completed agent result is different.
Both the evaluator and the audit function

00:01:23.826 --> 00:01:28.096
need that result, but their replicas share
the work within each function.

00:01:29.750 --> 00:01:34.479
One replica in each deployment handles
this delivery. Each function acknowledges

00:01:34.479 --> 00:01:39.639
independently. Delivery is at least once,
so handlers should tolerate seeing the

00:01:39.639 --> 00:01:40.929
same result again.
